WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.340
Bash SSH automation frequently transfers files.

2
00:00:04.440 --> 00:00:12.760
A script may place artifacts, fetch logs, copy configuration, stage updates, or collect diagnostic bundles.

3
00:00:12.860 --> 00:00:15.660
File transfer is not just movement of bytes.

4
00:00:15.760 --> 00:00:22.960
It is a state-changing operation that can affect permissions, ownership, availability, and later remote behavior.

5
00:00:23.240 --> 00:00:27.720
Source and destination paths should be validated before transfer.

6
00:00:27.820 --> 00:00:36.060
A script should know what it is allowed to read, where it is allowed to write, and what should happen if a path is missing or already exists.

7
00:00:36.160 --> 00:00:41.440
Avoid assumptions that depend on the current working directory unless that directory is set deliberately.

8
00:00:41.720 --> 00:00:45.480
Important changes should be staged before replacement where practical.

9
00:00:45.580 --> 00:00:52.780
Staging allows checks before a final handoff, reduces the chance of partial updates, and gives operators clearer failure points.

10
00:00:52.880 --> 00:00:58.800
It also supports safer rollback or cleanup behavior when a transfer does not complete.

11
00:00:59.080 --> 00:01:01.560
Ownership and permissions matter.

12
00:01:01.660 --> 00:01:06.540
A transferred file should not become broadly readable or executable by accident.

13
00:01:06.640 --> 00:01:15.440
Sensitive files should not be exposed through temporary locations, build workspaces, logs, or overly broad remote permissions.

14
00:01:15.540 --> 00:01:19.620
The script should handle confidentiality as part of the transfer design.

15
00:01:19.900 --> 00:01:21.900
Avoid accidental overwrites.

16
00:01:22.000 --> 00:01:26.640
If replacement is intended, make it deliberate and logged at a safe level.

17
00:01:26.740 --> 00:01:31.380
If replacement is not intended, the script should detect that condition and stop clearly.

18
00:01:31.480 --> 00:01:36.200
Silent overwrites are hard to troubleshoot and can damage operational confidence.

19
00:01:36.480 --> 00:01:38.880
Transfer outcomes should be checked.

20
00:01:38.980 --> 00:01:45.140
Operators need to know whether the transfer completed, which target was involved, and what high-level action occurred.

21
00:01:45.240 --> 00:01:52.600
Logs should avoid secrets and sensitive contents while still providing enough evidence for review and incident response.
